TSM tsx_amxx.dll patch -- Xash3D's bots count as bots
=======================================================

What this is: a binary patch to reTSX (ts\addons\amxmodx\modules\tsx_amxx.dll), the AMX Mod X
TS module that ships with reTS, so it puts Xash3D FWGS's bots in game the way it does
GoldSrc's.

tsx keeps its own table of who is in game, and every tsx native refuses a player that table
does not have ("[TSX] Player N is not ingame"). A bot reaches that table only through tsx's
ClientUserInfoChanged_Post, and only when the engine's GetPlayerAuthId for it is "BOT". GoldSrc
says "BOT". Xash3D FWGS says "ID_BOT" (engine/server/sv_client.c, SV_GetClientIDString), so on
Xash no bot was ever put in game: ts_setuserslots and every other tsx native failed on them,
and the plugin call setting a bot up died with a run time error.

The patch keeps the "BOT" test and adds one more: when the engine is Xash3D -- the cvar
host_ver exists, the same test ts_matchmode uses -- "ID_BOT" counts as well. On GoldSrc host_ver
does not exist and the answer is exactly what it was. AMX Mod X core needs nothing: its own bot
test checks FL_FAKECLIENT first, which FWGS sets.

Original file : tsx_amxx.dll.original (reTSX as shipped with reTS)
Patched file  : tsx_amxx_patched_idbot.dll
Original MD5  : d39732bce2d27488b7bbb0fa6996cd22
Patched MD5   : 9d8b6778605a0c52eaea28d67acf4dd2
Original size : 179200 bytes
Patched size  : 183296 bytes (+4096)

1. The hook
-------------------------------------------------------------

The bot test in ClientUserInfoChanged_Post, after the patch:

    100073cd  ff36             push dword ptr [esi]
    100073cf  ff15ccd50810     call dword ptr [0x1008d5cc]
    100073d5  83c404           add esp, 4
    100073d8  85c0             test eax, eax
    100073da  742f             je 0x1000740b
    100073dc  687b5f0210       push 0x10025f7b
    100073e1  50               push eax
    100073e2  e819ac0800       call 0x10092000
    100073e7  83c408           add esp, 8
    100073ea  85c0             test eax, eax
    100073ec  751d             jne 0x1000740b
    100073ee  89f1             mov ecx, esi
    100073f0  e84b9cffff       call 0x10001040

Only the call at RVA 0x73e2 changes:

    file offset 0x67e2:
      original: e8 b9 b8 00 00   (call strcmp)
      patched:  e8 19 ac 08 00   (call stub)

It is a relative call with no relocation entry, so it stays correct when the DLL is relocated.
The push of "BOT" before it carries a relocation and is left exactly as it was; the stub reads
that argument like strcmp would.

2. PE header changes -- registering the new section
-------------------------------------------------------------

  * File header, NumberOfSections: 5 -> 6
  * Optional header, SizeOfImage: 0x92000 -> 0x93000
  * New section table entry appended into existing header slack:

      Name             .h9bot
      VirtualAddress   0x92000
      VirtualSize      0x1000
      PointerToRawData 0x2bc00
      SizeOfRawData    0x1000
      Characteristics  0x60000020  (CODE | EXECUTE | READ -- not writable)

Existing sections are untouched. The new section is appended after the original last byte.

3. The new .h9bot section (file offset 0x2bc00, 4096 bytes)
-------------------------------------------------------------

The stub is at offset 0, "host_ver" at 0x80, "ID_BOT" at 0x90; the rest is zero.
It keeps strcmp's contract (cdecl, returns 0 for a bot) and is position independent:
`call L0 / pop ecx / sub ecx,0x92005` turns ecx into the module base, and the engine
function and both strings are reached as base+RVA, so it needs no relocation entries.

Stub disassembly (VA 0x10092000):

    10092000  e800000000       call 0x10092005
    10092005  59               pop ecx
    10092006  81e905200900     sub ecx, 0x92005
    1009200c  51               push ecx
    1009200d  ff74240c         push dword ptr [esp + 0xc]
    10092011  ff74240c         push dword ptr [esp + 0xc]
    10092015  e8860cf8ff       call 0x10012ca0
    1009201a  83c408           add esp, 8
    1009201d  59               pop ecx
    1009201e  85c0             test eax, eax
    10092020  7429             je 0x1009204b
    10092022  51               push ecx
    10092023  8d9180200900     lea edx, [ecx + 0x92080]
    10092029  52               push edx
    1009202a  ff9160d50800     call dword ptr [ecx + 0x8d560]
    10092030  83c404           add esp, 4
    10092033  59               pop ecx
    10092034  85c0             test eax, eax
    10092036  7414             je 0x1009204c
    10092038  8d9190200900     lea edx, [ecx + 0x92090]
    1009203e  52               push edx
    1009203f  ff742408         push dword ptr [esp + 8]
    10092043  e8580cf8ff       call 0x10012ca0
    10092048  83c408           add esp, 8
    1009204b  c3               ret 
    1009204c  b801000000       mov eax, 1
    10092051  c3               ret 

The steps are:

  * strcmp(auth, "BOT") through the module's own strcmp (RVA 0x12ca0). 0 is returned as is.
  * Otherwise g_engfuncs.pfnCVarGetPointer("host_ver"), from slot RVA 0x8d560 -- the slot
    reTSX itself uses at load (RVA 0x76c0) to look up its tsstats cvars.
  * NULL means GoldSrc: return 1, not a bot by auth -- the same answer as before.
  * Otherwise return strcmp(auth, "ID_BOT").

No real player's auth is exactly "ID_BOT": FWGS gives players ID_LOOPBACK, ID_LAN or ID_<hash>,
and GoldSrc's are STEAM_/VALVE_ forms.

